+    the human still confirms the decomposition at sign-off — the close fast path raises a
+    §6 NEEDS-HUMAN for exactly that (``driver._close_review_note``) — so a parent still
+    AWAITING_SIGNOFF is a split nobody has accepted yet, and driving its children would
+    spend a whole wave on work the next sign-off may reopen."""
+    if state.state(d) not in _TERMINAL:
+        return False
+    try:
+        return (d / state.CLOSE_MARKER).read_text(
+            encoding="utf-8").strip() == SPLIT_DISPOSITION
+    except OSError:
+        return False
+
+
+def _adoptable(cfg: Config, parent: Path, *, known: set[str]) -> list[Path]:
+    """The child bundles of a just-split ``parent`` that this run can drive.
+
+    The ids come from the parent's ``split-lineage.json`` (:func:`split.read_lineage`,
+    #456): a READ of a machine-readable record, never a parse of the prose breadcrumb
+    ``accept`` leaves in ``build-notes.md`` (``split.py:627-634``), which is written for a
+    human and is not a contract. A parent carrying the marker but no readable record is
+    reported and skipped — this degrades to today's behaviour (the operator's
+    ``pdca flow <child-ids>``), never a crash and never a guess.
+
+    Each id is then filtered exactly as :func:`flow_ids` filters an explicitly named one:
+    a bundle that does not exist or has no brief, one already terminal, and one already in
+    this run's drive set (``known``) is named and dropped. An id that does not resolve to a
+    direct child of the bundle root is dropped too — the record is a file an operator can
+    hand-edit, and ``cfg.bundle`` would happily build a path outside it (the hazard
+    ``split.validate`` guards at write time, ``split.py:290-311``).
